Absolute asymmetric synthesis refers to the synthesis of a chiral molecule where the absolute configuration of the product is determined and determined to be either R or S. This means the reaction produces only one enantiomer of the product, and the absolute configuration of that enantiomer is known. This is distinct from other types of asymmetric synthesis, where the reaction produces a mixture of enantiomers (e.g., 50:50) or the absolute configuration is not determined.  

For example, in **enantioselective synthesis**, the reaction produces a mixture of enantiomers, but the ratio of enantiomers is determined (e.g., 80:20). The absolute configuration of the enantiomers is not necessarily determined. In contrast, in **asymmetric synthesis**, the reaction produces a single enantiomer, but the absolute configuration is not determined.  

An example of **absolute asymmetric synthesis** is the **Nokin-Evans synthesis** of the natural alcohol **R-2-amino-3-methyl-1-pentanol** (a key intermediate in the synthesis of the antibiotic **vitamin D**). The reaction involves the use of a chiral catalyst (e.g., a chiral organometallic complex) to selectively control the stereochemistry of the reaction, leading to the formation of the R-enantiomer with high enantiomeric excess. The absolute configuration of the product is determined by X-ray crystallography or other methods.  

Thus, the key difference is that in absolute asymmetric synthesis, the absolute configuration of the product is determined, while in other types of asymmetric synthesis, the absolute configuration is either not determined or only the ratio of enantiomers is determined.  

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Absolute asymmetric synthesis: produces one enantiomer with known absolute configuration.} \\
&\text{Enantioselective synthesis: produces a mixture of enantiomers with a known ratio.} \\
&\text{Asymmetric synthesis: produces one enantiomer but without known absolute configuration.}
\end{aligned}
}

